  "body": "The account's ambiguity hypothesis remains the most directly grounded reading: the problem statement itself says the flatmates agree and later disagree about what the agreement meant. That is a specification gap. The rival does not refute this; it adds a second possible gap: not what the agreement meant, but whether any mutually binding agreement is wanted. The avoidance is compatible with either. So I would not choose one diagnosis. I would sequence a test that lets both show themselves. Revised plan: 1. No group meeting and no shared note yet. Talk separately to the other two, briefly, about two things: what they want the flat to be (a shared project with obligations, or overlapping private lives), and which one or two chores actually recur. Ask as a real question, not a survey. 2. Do not aggregate the answers immediately. If they diverge on what the flat is, treat that as the dispute the chores keep surfacing. If they agree, use the concrete chores. 3. Try a small trial on two or three items. Either tightened specification (what done means, by when, fallback) or a truce-shaped arrangement (owned zone, rotation with a default outcome nobody negotiates in the moment). Pick based on the conversation, not on a theory. Set a review date. 4. Watch what the withdrawing flatmate does with the proposal itself. If they engage with the content and recurrence falls, the ambiguity reading gains support. If they pull back from the very idea of a written or settled arrangement, the unit-versus-privacy reading gains support. If they disengage from both after one or two low-pressure attempts, stop optimizing chores and name the living-arrangement question. 5. Keep the ask small. Ten minutes on bins and kitchen is better than a talk about the whole house. But if the issue is the second axis, even a narrow ask can feel like the same imposition; that is why the separate conversations and the content-versus-proposal observation matter. 6. Avoid moral language, counting instances in the moment, and a single big settlement. Those convert a specification or unit question into a character dispute. What I would leave open: which reading is correct; whether load imbalance, outside stress, or conflict fatigue is the real driver; how long to persist before treating non-engagement as a signal about the lease. A review date is a practical substitute for a principled threshold, not an answer. I would reject the idea that either reading is established by this thin material, and I would reject using the rival's privacy reading as a blanket shield for not doing a fair share. The account's own objection is right that the specification move can treat a symptom; the rival's objection is right that the truce move can pathologize any attempt to make expectations explicit. Both objections should bind any plan. The selected input objection is absent, so there is no separate criticism to weigh. That absence limits confidence; it does not make the account or rival correct. The next move is small, separate, concrete, and reviewed.",
  "commitments": "{\"language\":\"FCL-1\",\"records\":[{\"id\":\"n1\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"The account's ambiguity hypothesis remains the best-supported starting point because the original problem reports later disagreement about what an agreement meant, but it should be treated as a hypothesis, not a diagnosis.\",\"mentions\":[\"daily/mini_fcl/cycle-1/account#c1\"]},{\"id\":\"n2\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"The rival's unit-versus-privacy axis is a genuine additional hypothesis: avoidance may be resistance to any settled mutual obligation, not only to vague terms. It is not established by the material.\",\"mentions\":[\"daily/mini_fcl/cycle-1/rival#r2\"]},{\"id\":\"n3\",\"type\":\"objection\",\"text\":\"The rival reading can become an unfalsifiable shield: any resistance to explicit expectations can be redescribed as a privacy concern, including unwillingness to share load. It needs the same testability as the ambiguity reading.\",\"target\":[\"n2\"],\"mentions\":[\"daily/mini_fcl/cycle-1/rival#r9\"]},{\"id\":\"n4\",\"type\":\"objection\",\"text\":\"The account's plan risks converting a relational or load problem into a specification problem, and starting with a shared note may trigger the withdrawal it aims to fix.\",\"target\":[\"n1\"],\"mentions\":[\"daily/mini_fcl/cycle-1/account#o1\"]},{\"id\":\"n5\",\"type\":\"problem\",\"text\":\"The material still lacks the recurring chores, duration, what the withdrawing flatmate has said, and whether load imbalance or outside stress is present. Sequencing remains guesswork.\"},{\"id\":\"n6\",\"type\":\"use\",\"text\":\"Start with separate low-pressure conversations with the other two flatmates, not a group meeting or shared note. Ask what they want the flat to be and which one or two chores recur most. Do not immediately aggregate into a household settlement.\",\"action\":\"Two one-to-one conversations; keep them separate.\",\"consequence\":\"Surfaces both axes at lower stakes; delays a single shared protocol.\",\"depends\":[\"n2\"]},{\"id\":\"n7\",\"type\":\"use\",\"text\":\"Run a small trial on two or three recurring items, using either tightened specifications or a truce-shaped arrangement (owned zone, rotation with default outcome), with a review date.\",\"action\":\"Choose based on the conversations, not theory.\",\"consequence\":\"Tests the ambiguity reading and the privacy reading in parallel.\",\"depends\":[\"n1\",\"n2\"]},{\"id\":\"n8\",\"type\":\"use\",\"text\":\"Observe whether the withdrawing flatmate engages with the content of the proposal or pulls back from the proposal itself. Content-engagement supports the ambiguity reading; pullback from the idea supports the unit-versus-privacy reading.\",\"depends\":[\"n2\"],\"mentions\":[\"daily/mini_fcl/cycle-1/rival#r7\"]},{\"id\":\"n9\",\"type\":\"commitment\",\"text\":\"If content-engagement and recurrence falls, continue tightening specifications. If pullback from the proposal persists, shift to truce-shaped arrangements and separate conversations. If both fail after two low-pressure attempts, name the living-arrangement question rather than repeating chore talks.\",\"depends\":[\"n6\",\"n7\",\"n8\"]},{\"id\":\"n10\",\"type\":\"commitment\",\"text\":\"Avoid moral language, counting instances in the moment, and a single household-wide settlement. Check each proposed remedy against the possibility that the other side experiences it as an unreasonable demand.\",\"mentions\":[\"daily/mini_fcl/cycle-1/account#o1\",\"daily/mini_fcl/cycle-1/rival#r9\"]},{\"id\":\"n11\",\"type\":\"problem\",\"text\":\"No threshold is given for how long to persist with low-pressure separate conversations before treating non-engagement as a signal about the living arrangement. A review date is a practical substitute, not a principled answer.\",\"depends\":[\"n9\"]},{\"id\":\"n12\",\"type\":\"use\",\"text\":\"Let the flatmates choose the review date and the first two recurring items, so the process itself is not an externally imposed specification.\",\"depends\":[\"n6\"]},{\"id\":\"n13\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"The selected input objection is absent, so no separate criticism was supplied. The objections available are internal to the account and rival and to this synthesis; this limits confidence but does not block a small test.\"}],\"uptake\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"n6\",\"n7\",\"n8\",\"n9\",\"n10\",\"n11\",\"n12\",\"n13\"]}"
}
